WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.980
The bedrock of Unreal's networking is replication.

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.140
So let's break down what replicates and when.

3
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.900
Simply put, variable replication is the process

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.740
of sending an authoritative value

5
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.439
of a variable over the network.

6
00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:18.559
Networked actors have got a net update frequency

7
00:00:18.559 --> 00:00:19.679
and this is used to determine

8
00:00:19.679 --> 00:00:22.320
how often an actor replicates.

9
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.519
The server keeps track of shadow data

10
00:00:24.519 --> 00:00:26.699
and this is essentially a copy of the last value

11
00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:28.699
that was sent to each client.

12
00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:30.440
The server compares the shadow data

13
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.759
against its current set of values

14
00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:33.880
and if there's a disparity,

15
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.719
we'll try and send an updated value down to that client,

16
00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:39.079
bringing it back in sync.

17
00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:41.799
This check comes with an inherent network cost.

18
00:00:41.799 --> 00:00:44.479
So if we don't anticipate this value changing very often,

19
00:00:44.479 --> 00:00:46.280
we can reduce the net update frequency

20
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.539
on a per actor basis.

21
00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:50.399
Managing the net update frequency rates

22
00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:51.759
is fairly low hanging fruit

23
00:00:51.759 --> 00:00:54.240
in regards to improving network performance.

24
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.759
Like most areas of engineering,

25
00:00:55.759 --> 00:00:59.409
if we can do something less, then we probably should.

26
00:00:59.409 --> 00:01:01.530
One element of networking that is often forgotten

27
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:05.400
is how do we handle clients joining midway through a game?

28
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.680
How Unreal handles replication for these new clients

29
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.760
is to simply send down the authoritative data

30
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.319
as soon as they connect.

31
00:01:11.319 --> 00:01:12.579
After all, their default values

32
00:01:12.579 --> 00:01:14.760
are likely to be out of sync with the server,

33
00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.950
thus triggering a disparity through a net update.

34
00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.269
Not all types can be replicated.

35
00:01:20.269 --> 00:01:21.510
Some are strongly supported,

36
00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.790
some absolutely are not strongly supported

37
00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.109
and some require a bit of nuance

38
00:01:25.109 --> 00:01:28.049
on the part of the developer to get them to play nice.

39
00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:30.489
So straight off the bat, we've got our atomic types,

40
00:01:30.489 --> 00:01:34.209
that being the bools, the ints and the floats.

41
00:01:34.209 --> 00:01:35.730
We also have support for structs

42
00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:37.310
that contain these atomic types.

43
00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:38.349
These can be sent over the network

44
00:01:38.349 --> 00:01:41.459
as a single block of data.

45
00:01:41.459 --> 00:01:42.980
Enums can also be replicated.

46
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.859
They take advantage of their underlying Uint8 typing

47
00:01:45.859 --> 00:01:46.920
to be sent over the network

48
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.939
and then are coerced back into their enum types

49
00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:52.219
on the other side.

50
00:01:52.219 --> 00:01:55.540
Arrays are also supported with only their changed elements

51
00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.120
being sent over the network at any one time.

52
00:01:58.120 --> 00:01:59.099
It's worth noting, however,

53
00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:01.340
that if you RPC down an array,

54
00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.019
the whole array will be sent,

55
00:02:03.019 --> 00:02:05.849
not just the changed elements.

56
00:02:05.849 --> 00:02:07.590
Unfortunately, there's no current support

57
00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.689
for serialization of TMaps.

58
00:02:09.689 --> 00:02:12.129
A very common and easy alternative to this data structure

59
00:02:12.129 --> 00:02:14.409
is to have an array of structs instead

60
00:02:14.409 --> 00:02:17.050
and replicate that array.

61
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.849
Pointers are supported for replication,

62
00:02:18.849 --> 00:02:20.590
but only when the thing it's pointing to

63
00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.849
is replicated in its own right.

64
00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:25.490
Under the hood, the pointer gets turned into a GUID.

65
00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.689
It's this GUID that then gets sent over the network

66
00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:29.889
in place of that pointer.

67
00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.590
When the GUID is received on the client,

68
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:32.729
we can then marry it back up

69
00:02:32.729 --> 00:02:34.770
to the actor that it once pointed to,

70
00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:38.719
rebuilding the pointer on the client.

71
00:02:38.719 --> 00:02:40.259
To make a variable replicated,

72
00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:42.580
we need to do the following.

73
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:43.620
Firstly, we need to make sure

74
00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.500
we're working with a replicated object,

75
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.939
and that can either be calling set replicated to true

76
00:02:47.939 --> 00:02:49.379
when working with an actor,

77
00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:51.819
typically called in the body of the constructor.

78
00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:54.020
Alternatively, we need to call set is replicated

79
00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.919
by default if we're working with a component.

80
00:02:56.919 --> 00:02:58.300
Finally, if we're working with a blueprint

81
00:02:58.300 --> 00:02:59.419
that supports replication,

82
00:02:59.419 --> 00:03:02.460
setting the be replicate flag to true in the class defaults

83
00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.490
will also cause this object to replicate.

84
00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.750
To actually flag a variable as replicated,

85
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:10.490
we have a couple of options.

86
00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:13.250
Firstly, we can add the replicated property specified

87
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:14.370
to the variable,

88
00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.409
and this is the most basic form of replication.

89
00:03:16.409 --> 00:03:18.210
When this variable changes server side,

90
00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:22.300
it's gonna propagate down to all of the relevant clients.

91
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.259
Alternatively, we can use the replicated

92
00:03:24.259 --> 00:03:25.900
using property specifier,

93
00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:27.860
and this will also propagate the change down,

94
00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.460
but it will also let developers specify a function to call

95
00:03:30.699 --> 00:03:32.219
once the value has arrived.

96
00:03:32.219 --> 00:03:34.180
In this example, when the property changes,

97
00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.039
the my func function is called,

98
00:03:36.039 --> 00:03:37.180
allowing us to drive gameplay

99
00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:40.030
as soon as we have an update.

100
00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.550
It's worth noting that a value will not be replicated

101
00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:44.530
if the value that you set it to on the server

102
00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:46.169
is the same as the value on the client,

103
00:03:46.169 --> 00:03:48.509
because it hasn't actually changed after all.

104
00:03:48.509 --> 00:03:50.770
Similarly, if your game can run in both single

105
00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.530
and multiplayer environments,

106
00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.849
we must be diligent in making sure critical gameplay

107
00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:57.509
isn't done in on rep functions.

108
00:03:57.509 --> 00:03:59.050
If we're running a single player game,

109
00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:02.099
the on rep isn't going to be called.

110
00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:03.419
Lastly, we need to override

111
00:04:03.419 --> 00:04:06.620
the get lifetime replicated props function of your class,

112
00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:08.159
flagging to the engine that this variable

113
00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:09.620
needs to be replicated.

114
00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:12.219
And we do this by employing the do rep lifetime macro

115
00:04:12.219 --> 00:04:14.229
as outlined above.

116
00:04:14.229 --> 00:04:16.829
Do rep lifetime is just the base macro.

117
00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:17.970
However, we can actually employ

118
00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:20.910
the do rep lifetime condition macro instead.

119
00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.250
This will let us specify a specific condition

120
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:24.829
that needs to be met before this variable

121
00:04:24.829 --> 00:04:27.670
is considered for replication.

122
00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.089
Something that is often forgotten

123
00:04:29.089 --> 00:04:30.329
when working with replication

124
00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.279
is that the actual act of replicating takes time.

125
00:04:34.279 --> 00:04:35.839
A client may hit a big patch of lag

126
00:04:35.839 --> 00:04:38.240
just after you send the variable over the network.

127
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:39.639
So it's critical we take this into account

128
00:04:39.639 --> 00:04:41.600
when we write our systems.

129
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.199
We might send two values over the network

130
00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:45.040
and have them arrive in a different order.

131
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.819
So we really need to waterproof our systems

132
00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:49.430
against common and easy gotchas like this.

133
00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:51.670
For instance, we can wrap our two variables in a struct

134
00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:53.550
and send that over the network instead,

135
00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:56.300
ensuring they arrive at the same time.

136
00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.300
Another critical thing we need to take into account

137
00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:02.350
is how well our clients deal with a lack of information.

138
00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:03.470
If we're moving something

139
00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:05.149
and replicating its new location,

140
00:05:05.149 --> 00:05:06.230
do we just stop moving it

141
00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:07.430
while we wait for the next packet?

142
00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:08.810
Of course not.

143
00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:10.750
We often want to consider elements of client side

144
00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:12.550
prediction when we work with larger systems

145
00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:14.050
to make client interactions

146
00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.470
feel more responsive and smooth.

147
00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:17.790
I'd highly recommend we take a look

148
00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.550
at the character movement component

149
00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:20.750
as a real standard example

150
00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:22.930
of how we can smooth out a client experience

151
00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.050
so the server is essentially invisible.
